{"vid":"Kq22wxqzJ7g","source":"Kq22wxqzJ7g.en-US.json3","lang":"en","cues":[{"t":1.3,"txt":"This video is brought to you by us,"},{"t":3.6,"txt":"RealPars!"},{"t":5.3,"txt":"Join the top 1% of PLC programmers."},{"t":8.6,"txt":"Learn from the world's best so you can join their rank."},{"t":11.9,"txt":"Head on over to realpars.com and start learning now"},{"t":17.5,"txt":"In this video, we're going to introduce you to a very common device"},{"t":21.5,"txt":"used in process control today – the Temperature Transmitter."},{"t":25.8,"txt":"We’ll discuss the sensors used with them,"},{"t":28.4,"txt":"and show you how they are connected in a typical control loop."},{"t":32.5,"txt":"We’ll also give you an insight into how they are calibrated."},{"t":36.8,"txt":"Temperature Transmitters are available in all different shapes and sizes"},{"t":41.2,"txt":"depending on the application and environment they are expected to operate."},{"t":46.4,"txt":"To name a few, there are:"},{"t":48.6,"txt":"DIN rail mounted temperature transmitters"},{"t":51.8,"txt":"Field mounted temperature transmitters, and"},{"t":54.7,"txt":"Hockey puck style temperature transmitters"},{"t":58.8,"txt":"Before we look at a Temperature Transmitter in detail,"},{"t":62.1,"txt":"let’s look at where it fits in a typical process control system."},{"t":67.2,"txt":"A Temperature Transmitter is a device"},{"t":69.5,"txt":"that converts the signal produced by a temperature sensor"},{"t":72.7,"txt":"into a standard instrumentation signal"},{"t":75.2,"txt":"representing a process variable temperature being measured and controlled."},{"t":79.9,"txt":"The most common transmitter instrumentation output signal is 4 to 20 mA."},{"t":86.8,"txt":"The signal from the Temperature Transmitter is sent to a Controller"},{"t":90.9,"txt":"that determines what action is required and generates an appropriate output signal."},{"t":96.3,"txt":"Controllers are either a PLC or a DCS in process control today."},{"t":102.6,"txt":"The Heating or Cooling source exerts a direct influence on the process"},{"t":107.1,"txt":"as directed by the controller."},{"t":110.1,"txt":"A Temperature Transmitter requires a power supply to operate."},{"t":114.3,"txt":"Most Temperature Transmitters are 2-wire types having only two wires."},{"t":119.2,"txt":"These two wires provide power for the transmitter and are also the signal lines!"},{"t":125.4,"txt":"A 4-wire transmitter has two wires connected to a power supply"},{"t":130.2,"txt":"and two signal wires connected to the PLC."},{"t":133.5,"txt":"The power supply can be AC or DC depending on the vendor and model."},{"t":139.9,"txt":"We haven’t said much about the temperature sensors,"},{"t":143,"txt":"so let’s do that now."},{"t":144.9,"txt":"The two most common temperature sensors used in process control are the thermocouple"},{"t":150.2,"txt":"and the Resistance Temperature Detector, or RTD."},{"t":154.7,"txt":"We’ve got lots of details on both of these devices in our other videos."},{"t":159.5,"txt":"Later, we’ll tell you where you can find these videos."},{"t":163.7,"txt":"The thermocouple produces a change in voltage with a temperature change."},{"t":168.7,"txt":"Thermocouples have only two wires."},{"t":171.9,"txt":"An RTD produces a change in resistance with a temperature change."},{"t":177.2,"txt":"Unlike a thermocouple that has only two leads,"},{"t":180.3,"txt":"an RTD can have two, three, or four leads."},{"t":185.3,"txt":"It’s common to see Temperature Transmitters"},{"t":187.9,"txt":"that are capable of working with either an RTD or a thermocouple."},{"t":193.1,"txt":"You may have heard the term \"Digital Temperature Transmitter\"."},{"t":197,"txt":"This term has been around for a while now and its meaning is evolving."},{"t":201.5,"txt":"Historically, a Digital Temperature Transmitter was a Smart Transmitter."},{"t":207.7,"txt":"The SMART transmitter is an intelligent transmitter"},{"t":211.2,"txt":"that has an analog output of 4 to 20 mA"},{"t":214.5,"txt":"and also provides digital communication signal information"},{"t":218.2,"txt":"such as Instrument Tag Names, Calibration Data, and Sensor Diagnostics."},{"t":224.3,"txt":"These SMART transmitters use HART protocol or FOUNDATION FIELDBUS or PROFIBUS."},{"t":231.9,"txt":"As with any instrumentation transmitter,"},{"t":234.8,"txt":"a Temperature Transmitter needs to be “adjusted” or “calibrated”"},{"t":238.9,"txt":"so that the current output of 4 to 20 mA"},{"t":241.9,"txt":"represents the temperature range of the process variable being measured."},{"t":246.7,"txt":"For example, a process variable temperature range of zero degrees Celsius"},{"t":251.6,"txt":"to 100 degrees Celsius will produce a current range of 4 to 20 mA."},{"t":257.9,"txt":"In every calibration scenario,"},{"t":260.3,"txt":"the sensor is replaced by a device that can simulate the output of the sensor"},{"t":264.7,"txt":"over the entire process variable temperature range."},{"t":269.2,"txt":"For a thermocouple,"},{"t":270.7,"txt":"the sensor is replaced by a device that produces a range of millivolts."},{"t":275.4,"txt":"As you can imagine, it’s not easy to create these small voltage"},{"t":280.9,"txt":"For an RTD, the sensor is replaced by a device that produces a range of resistances."},{"t":287,"txt":"Historically the device that produced a range of resistances was a decade box."},{"t":293.9,"txt":"Today we’ve got Signal Reference Calibrators"},{"t":296.9,"txt":"that can output a variety of electrical signals"},{"t":299.6,"txt":"that can simulate RTDs and Thermocouples"},{"t":302.7,"txt":"making it much easier to calibrate Temperature Transmitters."},{"t":306.8,"txt":"How are the actual adjustments performed?"},{"t":310.5,"txt":"Older style Temperature Transmitters"},{"t":312.7,"txt":"have multi-turn potentiometer adjustments called “Zero” and “Span”."},{"t":318.1,"txt":"The Zero pot is adjusted to produce 4 mA"},{"t":321.8,"txt":"when the simulated temperature is 0%,"},{"t":324.8,"txt":"and the Span pot is adjusted to produce 20 mA when the simulated temperature is 100%."},{"t":332,"txt":"As an example,"},{"t":333.3,"txt":"let’s calibrate a temperature transmitter connected to a Pt one-hundred RTD"},{"t":338.4,"txt":"assuming a temperature range of 32 degrees Fahrenheit to 100 degrees Fahrenheit."},{"t":344.3,"txt":"We can get the resistance values at our calibration temperatures"},{"t":348,"txt":"from a PT100 table."},{"t":350.5,"txt":"We will start with the Zero calibration."},{"t":353.2,"txt":"As you can see in the calibration table,"},{"t":355.7,"txt":"a PT100 RTD has a resistance of 100 ohms at 32°F,"},{"t":361.9,"txt":"so we’ll set 100 ohms on the decade box."},{"t":366,"txt":"The multimeter current should be adjusted to 4 mA"},{"t":369.6,"txt":"by rotating the Zero adjustment screw."},{"t":373.1,"txt":"For Span calibration, we'll find the high range temperature on the table"},{"t":377.7,"txt":"and set the corresponding resistance on the decade box."},{"t":381.4,"txt":"The multimeter current should be adjusted to 20 mA"},{"t":385,"txt":"by rotating the Span adjustment screw."},{"t":388.5,"txt":"Newer style Temperature Transmitters do not have external Zero or span adjustments"},{"t":394,"txt":"but are programmed or calibrated using the software."},{"t":397.5,"txt":"The Transmitter is connected to a computer via a serial USB connection."},{"t":403.8,"txt":"This video wouldn’t be complete"},{"t":405.8,"txt":"if we didn’t talk about one of the newest trends in Transmitters;"},{"t":409.4,"txt":"the Wireless Transmitter."},{"t":411.4,"txt":"As the name implies, the transmitter does not send its signal to a receiver via wires."},{"t":417.5,"txt":"The signal is transmitted to a receiver via a Wi-Fi signal."},{"t":422.5,"txt":"Once the signal is received, it can be sent to a computer"},{"t":426.4,"txt":"so readings can be saved and later printed or exported to a spreadsheet."},{"t":431.1,"txt":"Or, the received signal can be retransmitted via wires"},{"t":435.3,"txt":"as a 4 to 20 mA signal to a PLC."},{"t":439.8,"txt":"To learn more about temperature sensors,"},{"t":442.4,"txt":"you might want to review our videos called “What is a Temperature Sensor?”"},{"t":446.8,"txt":"and “Pt100 Sensor Explained”."},{"t":449.8,"txt":"You can find the link to these videos in the description."},{"t":454.3,"txt":"Let’s review what we’ve discussed…"},{"t":457.1,"txt":"Temperature Transmitters are available in several different shapes and sizes."},{"t":462.6,"txt":"A Temperature Transmitter is a device"},{"t":464.9,"txt":"that converts the signal produced by a temperature sensor"},{"t":467.9,"txt":"into a standard instrumentation signal"},{"t":470.5,"txt":"representing a process variable temperature being measured and controlled."},{"t":475.2,"txt":"The most common transmitter instrumentation output signal is 4 to 20 mA."},{"t":481.5,"txt":"Most Temperature Transmitters are 2-wire types having only two wires."},{"t":487.4,"txt":"The two most common temperature sensors used in process control"},{"t":491.4,"txt":"are the thermocouple and the Resistance Temperature Detector, or RTD."},{"t":497.6,"txt":"Many Temperature Transmitters are capable of"},{"t":500.1,"txt":"working with either an RTD or a thermocouple."},{"t":504.5,"txt":"Signal Reference Calibrators can output a variety of electrical signals that"},{"t":508.8,"txt":"can simulate RTDs and Thermocouples making it much easier to perform calibration."},{"t":515.4,"txt":"Older style Temperature Transmitters have multi-turn potentiometer adjustments"},{"t":520.3,"txt":"called Zero and Span used for calibration."},{"t":525.2,"txt":"Newer style Temperature Transmitters"},{"t":527.3,"txt":"are programmed or calibrated using the software."},{"t":531.9,"txt":"A wireless Temperature Transmitter sends signals to a receiver via a Wi-Fi signal."},{"t":540.7,"txt":"Want to learn PLC programming in an easy-to-understand format?"},{"t":545.3,"txt":"and take your career to the next level?"},{"t":547.8,"txt":"Head on over to realpars.com"}],"words":[{"t":1.281,"w":"This"},{"t":1.281,"w":"video"},{"t":1.281,"w":"is"},{"t":1.281,"w":"brought"},{"t":1.281,"w":"to"},{"t":1.281,"w":"you"},{"t":1.281,"w":"by"},{"t":1.281,"w":"us,"},{"t":3.563,"w":"RealPars!"},{"t":5.279,"w":"Join"},{"t":5.279,"w":"the"},{"t":5.279,"w":"top"},{"t":5.279,"w":"1%"},{"t":5.279,"w":"of"},{"t":5.279,"w":"PLC"},{"t":5.279,"w":"programmers."},{"t":8.567,"w":"Learn"},{"t":8.567,"w":"from"},{"t":8.567,"w":"the"},{"t":8.567,"w":"world's"},{"t":8.567,"w":"best"},{"t":8.567,"w":"so"},{"t":8.567,"w":"you"},{"t":8.567,"w":"can"},{"t":8.567,"w":"join"},{"t":8.567,"w":"their"},{"t":8.567,"w":"rank."},{"t":11.862,"w":"Head"},{"t":11.862,"w":"on"},{"t":11.862,"w":"over"},{"t":11.862,"w":"to"},{"t":11.862,"w":"realpars.com"},{"t":11.862,"w":"and"},{"t":11.862,"w":"start"},{"t":11.862,"w":"learning"},{"t":11.862,"w":"now"},{"t":17.481,"w":"In"},{"t":17.481,"w":"this"},{"t":17.481,"w":"video,"},{"t":17.481,"w":"we're"},{"t":17.481,"w":"going"},{"t":17.481,"w":"to"},{"t":17.481,"w":"introduce"},{"t":17.481,"w":"you"},{"t":17.481,"w":"to"},{"t":17.481,"w":"a"},{"t":17.481,"w":"very"},{"t":17.481,"w":"common"},{"t":17.481,"w":"device"},{"t":21.497,"w":"used"},{"t":21.497,"w":"in"},{"t":21.497,"w":"process"},{"t":21.497,"w":"control"},{"t":21.497,"w":"today"},{"t":21.497,"w":"–"},{"t":21.497,"w":"the"},{"t":21.497,"w":"Temperature"},{"t":21.497,"w":"Transmitter."},{"t":25.79,"w":"We’ll"},{"t":25.79,"w":"discuss"},{"t":25.79,"w":"the"},{"t":25.79,"w":"sensors"},{"t":25.79,"w":"used"},{"t":25.79,"w":"with"},{"t":25.79,"w":"them,"},{"t":28.415,"w":"and"},{"t":28.415,"w":"show"},{"t":28.415,"w":"you"},{"t":28.415,"w":"how"},{"t":28.415,"w":"they"},{"t":28.415,"w":"are"},{"t":28.415,"w":"connected"},{"t":28.415,"w":"in"},{"t":28.415,"w":"a"},{"t":28.415,"w":"typical"},{"t":28.415,"w":"control"},{"t":28.415,"w":"loop."},{"t":32.512,"w":"We’ll"},{"t":32.512,"w":"also"},{"t":32.512,"w":"give"},{"t":32.512,"w":